  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High confidence for rain tonight then more scattered \nin nature Thursday into Friday.\n\nLow pressure has moved into the Great Lakes with a cold front\ntrailing south through the OH/TN Valleys. Conveyor belt of\nmoisture aligned with front will move east and across the\nmountains tonight...the front moving into the Piedmont Thursday\nbefore stalling. A secondary wave of low pressure is forecast to\ndevelop along the front Friday providing the Piedmont and\nTidewater areas with some rain before the front moves off the \ncoast Friday night. \n\nIn general, expecting two to five tenths (0.20 to 0.50) \nrainfall across the mountains and foothills of the Blue Ridge \ntonight before tapering to showers Thursday. Another round of \nrain will then develop over the Piedmont late Thursday night \nbefore moving northeast and through the Tidewater of VA/NC \nFriday. Rain amounts Friday will be mainly confined to the \nPiedmont with model ensembles suggesting little or no rain for\nthe mountains, one to two tenths (0.10 to 0.20) for LYH/DAN \nnear Highway 29 and trending upwards to near one inch (0.75 to\n1.25) of rain just east of Farmville and South Boston. Once the\nfront moves off the coast Friday, look for a drying trend this \nweekend and into next week. \n\nAs far as thunderstorms, models entertain enough instability to\nsupport a rumble of thunder tonight, but nothing severe. Better\nsupport will reside southwest of our CWA over the southern\nAppalachians. That said, if deeper convection does enter the\nCWA, then look for somewhat higher QPF. Not out of the question\nto see 0.75 to 1.50 rainfall where there is more instability \ndriven activity. Convective allowing models, such as the HRRR, \nindicate this potential over the NC High Country with Beech Mtn \nthrough Mtn Empire in VA (including Mount Rogers) having a \nchance at exceeding 1.00 inch of QPF tonight. The Weather \nPrediction Center has this area highlighted for marginal risk \nof excessive rainfall (5% chance that rainfall exceeds flash \nflood guidance)...so the potential is justified.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Unseasonably warm temperatures continue through \nFriday, returning to near normal this weekend. \n\nSouth winds ahead of the approaching front will maintain mild\nconditions with above normal temperatures. Clouds will mute the\ntemperature rise Thursday and Friday, but still favoring mild\nconditions due to the elevated dewpoints. Once the front moves\noff the coast Friday, cold/dry advection will set in for Friday\nnight and continue through the weekend. This will promote much \ncooler temperatures with reading returning to near normal.",
